## SQ-PWG-28 | power resistor 1 ohm at 40 V (1.6 kW vs 1 kW) overheats into an Overheated Component; loop open after
@group A
@commands PWG-13 (D4), Z 1040: `zpwg3 src 1100 -60 1040 40`; `zpwg3 place 1104 -60 1040 powergrid:power_resistor facing=down`; `zpwg3 scroll 1104 -60 1040 9` (1 ohm: the power resistor's scale is (i % 9 + 1) x 10^(i / 9 - 1); default index 0 is 0.1 ohm, which would burn the copper wires at 400 A); 40 V on 1 ohm is 40 A (copper wire is rated 80 A) and 1.6 kW against the resistor's 1 kW rating; wires source T0 to resistor T0, resistor T1 to source T1; `zpwg3 be 1104 -60 1040` every 20 ticks for 400 ticks; then `zpwgg overheated 1104 -60 1040 1`
@pass `thermal T=` rises, smoke particles server side are not checked; the resistor turns into an Overheated Component: `[zpwgg] overheated at 1104 -60 1040 original=Block{powergrid:power_resistor}` with data keys; no explosion, no ERROR; the loop is open afterwards (`zpwg3 be 1100 -60 1040`: the source still holds T0 = 40 V, T1 = 0 V, as a voltage source does; the wires to the component carry no current)
@fails `ThermalBehaviour.explode` / `overheatBlock`; power resistor rating `CThermal.maxPower(1000, 2.0)`; if it never overheats, record P = V^2 / R from `be` (terminal voltages)
