## CQ-PWG-24
@group client
@commands PWG-41 by hand: client boot with the `-14` jar (client mods plus the jar; never `mods/` of the instance) joining SQ-PWG-72's server (survival, op). `/give @s powergrid:circuit_design_table`, `create:empty_schematic 4`, `powergrid:wire_connector 4`, `powergrid:resistor 4`, `powergrid:diode 2`, `powergrid:lv_switch 2`, `powergrid:display_module`, `powergrid:copper_cord 4`, `powergrid:socket`. Place the table, right-click it unpowered; power it from a socket and a 130 V source (as SQ-PWG-73, or by hand with the cord); right-click again. Table screen: hover the board area, click it. Editor: click a Wire Connector in the inventory, place two; press R while placing a resistor, place it; hotbar key picks a part; T and draw traces from the connectors to the resistor pads (a single click on a pad, then the end; shift inverts the continue rule); X and draw one trace on the back layer; D and delete an area; S and click the resistor: the properties panel, type 220 in its value and press Enter, click the display module part's option and scroll it; hover a pad (pad tooltip), hover the property name (summary); type a name in the name field; right-click a part (removes it), press Esc (warning), place it back; confirm. Table screen again: put an Empty Schematic in the save slot, press confirm; take the output, put it in the load slot, press the arrow. Open the folder button screen: type `cq24`, save, save again (overwrite popup, then confirm), cancel, reopen, load `cq24`. Break the power (remove the source) while in the editor. Screenshots of the three screens (`RES/shots/CQ-PWG-24_<screen>.png`); `ls <client dir>/circuits/`
@pass unpowered survival: action bar "This device needs power", no screen; powered: the table glows (block light, additive glow) and opens "Circuit Designer" with the board preview and three slots plus the inventory; the editor shows the 8x grid, the part follows the mouse with a green / red outline and pad numbers, R turns it (sound), traces draw on the front in white and on the back half-transparent, D clears, S selects with a yellow outline and the properties panel on the left (title the part's name, the value field takes 220 on Enter, the option scroll input cycles names); tooltips on pads and property names; right-click removal then Esc shows the red "not saved" line (B24); confirm shows the saving overlay and returns to the table screen with the design drawn; the confirm button turns the blank into a "Circuit Schematic" named as typed (the blank is used up); the arrow loads it back; `circuits/cq24.nbt` exists and loads back the same design; losing power closes the editor after saving it; no exception with `org.patryk3211.powergrid`, `circuits` or `GuiGraphics` in the client log
@fails crash on open: the screen class in the client log (`CircuitDesignTableEditScreen`, a widget's `renderButton`), send its first 20 frames; blank or misplaced panels: `utility/GuiGraphics.blitRepeating` / `blit` argument order; tooltips missing: `getTooltipFromItem`, `renderTooltip`; typed value lost: `TextFieldPropertyWidget.mouseClicked` (focus read before the click)
@draft no batch holds this row yet
## CQ-PWG-25
@group client
@commands By hand, creative, on CQ-PWG-24's server: `/zpwg9 board X Y Z resistor` near the player (or the CQ-PWG-24 board), empty hands, right-click it (not sneaking); in the editor rename it and change the resistor value, confirm; pick-block the board and read its tooltip. JEI: open the editor again, drag a Resistor from the JEI list onto the grid and place it; check the button tooltips name the bound keys (T, D, S, X); change the display module option (enum) and press Esc. Survival: take a Circuit Schematic, hover it (no creative line); creative: hover it (line "Sneak right-click to convert into an assembled circuit"), sneak right-click it (becomes a Circuit Board item), place it. Client config `HighContrastTraces = true` (`config/powergrid-client.toml`, `[circuit]`), reopen the editor
@pass empty-hand right-click opens the board editor titled with the board's name in the name field; confirm closes it and the board takes the new name (tooltip "Schematic: <name>"; B22) and the new value (the circuit current changes); the JEI drag starts placement of the dragged part; tooltips show the keys; the option change then Esc shows the "not saved" warning (B24); the creative line shows only in creative; the converted board places with the design; high contrast draws the front red and the back green; no client log exception
@fails right-click does nothing: `CircuitBoardBlock.use` (creative, both hands empty); JEI drag ignored: `PowerGridJEI.registerGuiHandlers` (JEI 11 `getTargets`), the item in `#powergrid:circuit_component`
@draft no batch holds this row yet
@@text
@@text **P9c update 2026-11-13 (etching, acid, assembly):** jar
@@text `jar_archive/pwg_gate/backportedPowerGrid-0.6.2-ce-19.2-15.jar` (sha1 f95311cc26f8294fe100347ca2d841af193de4dd; port
@@text phase **9.3**) supersedes `-14` for every row from here on (the P2 to P9b rows stay valid on it; no change to earlier
@@text code). It adds the Blazing Acid fluid (`powergrid:acid`, `powergrid:flowing_acid`, liquid block `acid`, item
@@text `acid_bucket`; 2 HP of `acid` damage on a third of the ticks a living entity spends in it), the items `empty_circuit`,
@@text `unetched_circuit`, `incomplete_unetched_circuit` (transitional) and `incomplete_circuit` (one per stack, takes the
@@text design's parts from mechanical arms), 6 recipes (100 in all: `mixing/acid`, `mixing/etched_circuit_board`,
@@text `sequenced_assembly/unetched_circuit_board`, `cutting/empty_circuit_slabs`, `deploying/schematic_copy`,
@@text `sequenced_assembly/battery`), 4 mixins (`BeltDeployerMixin`: the deployer's held design is copied onto the
@@text schematic it makes; `RecipeApplierMixin`: deployers and presses carry the design through the unetched circuit's
@@text assembly; `ArmInteractionPointMixin`: an arm puts a part into an incomplete circuit on a depot or belt, one at a time,
@@text the last part makes the Circuit Board; `ArmBlockEntityMixin`: an incomplete circuit on a belt an arm deposits to stops
@@text at that segment), and the JEI categories Circuit Designing (design table) and Circuit Assembly (mechanical arm). Fixes
@@text B25 (an arm's later belt outputs were not checked when its first output was not a belt) and B26 (incomplete circuit
@@text tooltip / bar with a part the synced table lacks). New probe `tools/scratch_query/pwg_p9c_probe.js`
@@text (`zz_pwg_p9c_probe.js`, `zpwg9c`, commands in its header; fake player `[zpwg9c]`; builds real Create machines:
@@text creative motors, belts, deployers, press, spout, basins on creative blaze burners, mechanical arms). Area: X 1135 to
@@text 1200, Z 1855 to 1915, Y -60 (`forceload add 1135 1855 1200 1915`). Source design: `zpwg9 board 1140 -60 1858
@@text resistor` (two wire connectors and a 100 ohm resistor: 3 parts), made in SQ-PWG-76 and read by every chain row.
@@text Survival chain order: SQ-PWG-78 (schematic copy) -> SQ-PWG-79 (unetched circuit) -> SQ-PWG-80 (acid and etching) ->
@@text SQ-PWG-81 (arm assembly, the board); run them in that order on one boot (each takes the previous row's item).
@@text
@@text **Run order (high priority first):** SQ-PWG-76 (boot, registries, mixins, recipes), CQ-PWG-26 (client: fluid
@@text render, JEI categories, tooltips; client crash risk), SQ-PWG-77 (acid placement, flow, damage, bucket), SQ-PWG-78 to
@@text -81 (the survival chain from schematic to etched board), SQ-PWG-82 (arm on a belt, B25 lock), SQ-PWG-83 (battery
